    "message": {
     "content": "### The Verdict: **Do Not Accept As-Is \u2014 Negotiate**\n\nBecause you are happy at Meta and Google recruited you passively, **you hold all the leverage**. \n\nAt first glance, the offer looks competitive, but when you look closely at the mechanics, **it is a low-to-mid anchor for a Google L6 PM**:\n\n---\n\n### Key Issues with the Current Offer\n\n#### 1. You Are Taking an Immediate $25k Cut in Base Salary\n* **Current Meta Base:** $240,000\n* **Google Offer Base:** $215,000 *(Range: $183,000 \u2013 $271,000)*\n* Offering you $215,000 places you in the lower half of Google\u2019s L6 band. Moving laterally from Meta IC6 to Google L6 should never involve a pay cut in guaranteed base cash.\n\n#### 2. The 4-Year Annualized Math is Lower Than Your Current TC\n* **Your Current Meta TC:** ~$526,000/yr (with $200k in stock vesting over the next 12 months alone).\n* **Google 4-Year Average (Straight-Line):**\n  * Base: $215,000\n  * Bonus (20% target): $43,000\n  * Equity ($800k / 4): $200,000/yr\n  * **Average Annual TC: $458,000** *(a ~$68k/yr drop from what you make today)*.\n\n#### 3. The Front-Loaded Vesting Schedule Creates a Steep Year 3/4 Cliff\nGoogle typically vests new-hire equity on a **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** schedule:\n* **Year 1:** $215k + $43k + $304k (38% of $800k) = **$562,000** *(decent)*\n* **Year 2:** $215k + $43k + $256k (32% of $800k) = **$514,000**\n* **Year 3:** $215k + $43k + $160k (20% of $800k) = **$418,000** *(sharp drop)*\n* **Year 4:** $215k + $43k + $80k (10% of $800k) = **$338,000** *(severe drop)*\n\nWhile annual refreshes help soften this, **refresher grants are performance-dependent and not guaranteed**. You should never take a lower initial grant relying solely on future refreshes to keep your compensation level.\n\n#### 4. No Sign-On Bonus\nBig tech lateral moves at L6/IC6 routinely include a sign-on bonus ($50,000 \u2013 $100,000+) to offset unvested equity left behind, potential performance rating resets, and transition friction.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer Targets)\n\n| Component | Current Meta | Initial Google Offer | **Counter Target** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $215,000 | **$245,000 \u2013 $250,000** |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $43,000 (20%) | **$49,000 \u2013 $50,000** *(scales with base)* |\n| **Equity (GSUs)**| ~$250,000/yr | $800,000 / 4 yrs | **$950,000 \u2013 $1,000,000** / 4 yrs |\n| **Sign-on Bonus**| N/A | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** |\n\n*Target Year 1 TC with counter:* **~$680,000 \u2013 $710,000** (inclusive of sign-on).  \n*Target 4-Year Annualized Run Rate:* **~$535,000 \u2013 $550,000/yr**.\n\n---\n\n### Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nCommunicate appreciation for the team and role, but state clearly that you are walking away from a well-compensated, established position at Meta and need the package adjusted to make sense financially.\n\n#### Email / Call Framework:\n\n> *\"Thank you for extending the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team and am excited about the scope of the role.*\n>\n> *That said, compensation is a critical factor for me. Since I was not actively looking and am in a strong position at Meta, this offer as structured doesn\u2019t make financial sense for a lateral move:*\n>\n> 1. * **Base Salary:** My current base at Meta is $240,000. Taking a step down to $215,000 isn't viable. Given the posted range goes up to $271,000, I would need base salary calibrated at **$250,000**.*\n> 2. * **Equity:** On a standard 4-year annualized basis, $800,000 represents a step backward from my current equity run-rate and unvested trajectory. To bridge this and protect against the Year 3/4 drop-off, I am looking for **$1,000,000 in GSUs**.*\n> 3. * **Sign-on Bonus:** To offset the unvested equity and performance cycle timing I would forfeit by leaving Meta mid-stream, I am asking for a **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000 sign-on bonus**.*\n>\n> *If we can get the total package to this level, I would be thrilled to sign and join the team.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n* **Do not accept** the initial offer.\n* **Negotiate firmly** on base ($245k+), equity ($950k\u2013$1M), and a sign-on bonus ($75k+).\n* **Walk away** if Google refuses to meaningfully bridge the gap\u2014you are in a strong position at Meta, and moving for less guaranteed value carries unnecessary career and compensation risk.",
